{"id":"hijacking-in-ten-club-period-five-feeds","name":"Hijacking in ten club period five feeds","chapter":"Other","kind":"transition","page":209,"label":null,"jugglers":null,"objects":10,"timing":null,"pdf":"book/highgate.pdf#page=209&view=FitH,774","passist":null,"passist_candidate":null,"passist_fork":null,"notations":[],"derived":null,"body":[{"t":"p","h":"You can switch between different ten club period five feeds using the rules for hijacking in period five patterns:"},{"t":"p","h":"1.You can throw a club to a hand that is expecting to receive a zip. Let&#x27;s call this the hijack club."},{"t":"p","h":"2.If someone throws a hijack club to you, then you have a club that you wanted to zip but now can&#x27;t. You should throw this &#x27;problem&#x27; club to the hand that threw the hijack club."},{"t":"p","h":"3.If there is a club &#x27;missing&#x27; from your pattern, you should zip to avoid a gap."},{"t":"p","h":"Also you may need to add transition throws to lo-jack a pattern."},{"t":"p","h":"The following diagram shows some possible hijacks between ten club period five feeds. The letters between the feeds show who does the hijacking to switch between the patterns: &#x27;A&#x27; for Anne; &#x27;B&#x27; for Ben and &#x27;C&#x27; for Clare. The transition with the letter in a square is not a hijack. In this cases Clare switches between a not why and a why not? (or vice-versa). The patterns for the other jugglers do not change."},{"t":"d","i":0}],"drawings":[{"file":"p209-1.png","kind":"transition map","size":[982,1082],"svg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 982 1082' style='position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:100%;height:100%' preserveAspectRatio='none'><rect x='13' y='15' width='460' height='183' rx='12' fill='none' stroke='#868686' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='506' y='15' width='459' height='183' rx='12' fill='none' stroke='#868686' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='13' y='232' width='460' height='183' rx='12' fill='none' stroke='#868686' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='506' y='232' wid